            <div xml:id="d138">

                <pb facs="0320" n="288" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v10/pg288"/>

                <head>138. Telegram From the Commander in Chief, Atlantic (Dennison) to the Chief of
                    Naval Operations (Burke)<anchor n="0" xml:id="d138-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Norfolk</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1961-04-19T02:01:00-05:00">April 19, 1961,
                            2:01 a.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>190701Z. Exclusive for Admiral Burke from Dennison.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>A.</label>
                    <item>Your 190137Z.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d138-f2"/></item>
                    <label>B.</label>
                    <item>My 190627Z.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d138-f3"/>
                        <list>
                            <label>1.</label>
                            <item>See Ref B to CTG 81.8 info JCS.</item>
                            <label>2.</label>
                            <item>Evacuation of wounded is completely out of the question without
                                overt involvement of US forces. Furthermore, I know of no haven in
                                    <pb facs="0321" n="289" xml:id="pg289"/> some place
                                “inaccessible to news hawks.” Evacuation either by day or night is a
                                fantastically unrealistic project unless I am permitted to put
                                sufficient force ashore, with air and gunfire support from the sea,
                                to provide a beach head. Alternative would be for CEF ships to bring
                                wounded from beach to sea.</item>
                        </list></item>
                </list>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
